#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# lane-rename — a lane is renamed by ONE WORD, in ONE COMMIT, and its old name
# keeps resolving for ever.
#
#   lane-rename <old> <new> ["<why>"]   rename the lane: the row, its object
#                                       log, its handoff and lanes/aliases.tsv
#                                       in one commit; then this window's name
#                                       and this session's name
#   lane-rename <old> <new> --verbatim  <new> need not be `<repo>-<n>`
#   lane-rename <old> <new> --no-github skip the per-object comments
#   lane-rename <old> <new> --no-tmux   write only; touch no window and no pane
#
# lane-collision-protocol **Amendment 16**, ratified 2026-09-14T09:24:35Z
# verbatim *"Ratify as drafted (Recommended)"*, on Brett Heap's request of the
# same day, verbatim: *"we need the ability to rename a lane. maybe lanes
# --rename <current lane name> <new lane name>."* The spelling he chose is this
# one — *"lane-rename <old> <new> (Recommended)"* — and `lanes` stays read-only
# (clause (h), and Amendment 11(j) before it).
#
# WHY IT EXISTS: TWO RENAMES BY HAND IN ONE NIGHT. The register's rows are keyed
# by lane name; so are `lanes/log/<lane>.md`, the handoff
# `session-handoff-<date>-lane-<name>.md`, the tmux window, the session name
# (Amendment 12's lock), every Rule 6 line's attribution, every other lane's log
# line that names it, and the wire form `Lane: <lower> (<Canonical>)` on every
# PR, issue and comment the lane ever wrote. Nothing renamed any of it. On
# 2026-09-14 two lanes renamed themselves anyway, each as one `RENAMED` line
# appended to `LANES.md` — *"the lines are honest and they are all there is"*.
#
# WHAT THIS COMMAND IS, AND WHAT IT IS NOT. The WRITE is
# `lanes-edit.sh rename-lane`, which is where every refusal and all four moves
# live: this file adds nothing to the register and nothing to any log. What it
# adds is the two names a register writer may not touch (clause (f)):
#
#   * THE WINDOW, renamed with `tmux rename-window` when this window carries the
#     old name — one tmux call any client may make, which is why `lane-start`
#     makes exactly this one at every launch;
#   * THE SESSION, whose name is LOCKED to the lane by Amendment 12(h) and which
#     no API renames from outside it. The one path is `/rename <new>` typed into
#     the lane's own pane (M1), and the conditions on typing at all are M1's: a
#     pane whose current command is `claude`, and nothing else. Where they do not
#     hold, the line is PRINTED — a lock that silently did nothing is a lock
#     nobody knows is broken.
#
# RUN FROM ELSEWHERE IT STILL RENAMES THE LANE, and says what is left: clause
# (f) is explicit that *"the window and session are the lane's to fix at its
# next prompt, and the guard says so"*. Amendment 12's name guard does exactly
# that, out of the same alias table: at the lane's next prompt it resolves the
# window's former name to the lane's row, renames the window itself, and types
# the `/rename`. So a rename made from another window is finished by the lane
# without anybody typing anything.
#
# AND IT CANNOT BE RUN OVER A RUNNING LANE FROM OUTSIDE IT. Clause (a) refuses a
# rename while a LIVE session holds `<old>` in another window, and that refusal
# is the helper's, before anything is written — because the running session's
# own name would then be a lane the register no longer carries, and that is the
# state the name guard refuses every prompt over.
#
# EXIT CODES ARE THE HELPER'S, passed straight through: `0` done · `1` no
# register, or a read that failed · `2` a refusal (clause (a), bad arguments, or
# a `lanes-edit.sh` predating this amendment) · `3` the commit is local and this
# attempt cannot confirm it reached origin · `4` the mutex · `5` a move refused
# · `6` git. Never this file's own numbers: every one of them is decided in the
# writer, and two tables of codes for one act is how they come to disagree.
#
#   $LANES_EDIT          the writer (default: lanes-edit.sh beside this file)
#   $LANE_RENAME_NO_TMUX 1: the same as --no-tmux (the test seam)

set -uo pipefail

prog="${0##*/}"
SELF="${BASH_SOURCE[0]:-$0}"
selfdir="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$SELF")" && pwd)"

say()  { printf '%s\n' "$*"; }
step() { printf '%s: %s\n' "$prog" "$*" >&2; }
note() { printf '%s: %s\n' "$prog" "$*" >&2; }
die()  { printf '\n%s: REFUSED: %s\n' "$prog" "$1" >&2; exit "${2:-2}"; }
# `tr`, not `${x,,}`: every file this repository ships is parsed under macOS
# bash 3.2 in CI, where `${x,,}` is a syntax error.
lc()   { printf '%s' "${1-}" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'; }

usage() {
	cat <<'USAGE'
lane-rename <old> <new> ["<why>"] [--verbatim] [--no-github] [--no-tmux]

Rename a lane. ONE COMMIT, refused or whole (lane-collision-protocol Amendment
16): the register row's key cell, `lanes/log/<old>.md` → `lanes/log/<new>.md`
with a RENAMED line, the handoff the row names → the same date with
`lane-<new>` plus a Rule 3 stamp, and `lanes/aliases.tsv` gains `<old> <new>`.
Then one GitHub comment on every object the lane holds.

THE OLD NAME KEEPS RESOLVING, for ever and in every reader: `who`, `lanes`,
`live-holder`, `lane-start`, Rule 6 attribution, the SessionStart block and the
name guard all resolve a lane name through `lanes/aliases.tsv` before they read.

  --verbatim   accept a <new> that is not of the form <repo>-<n>. Rule 4's form
               is what `lanes --prefix`, the next free position and
               `lane-start <repo> <n>` are computed from, so a lane renamed out
               of it loses all three; Amendment 11's `--dir` lanes are the
               deliberate exception this flag exists for.
  --no-github  make the commit and post no comment on any object the lane holds.
  --no-tmux    write only: rename no window and type into no pane.
  -h, --help   this.

Run it IN THE LANE'S OWN WINDOW and the whole act is one command: the window is
renamed and `/rename <new>` is typed into the pane. Run from anywhere else it
renames the lane and says what is left — and the name guard finishes the window
and the session at that lane's next prompt.

A rename is REFUSED, before anything is written, where: there is no row under
<old> in any case; a row exists under <new> in any case; <new> is not <repo>-<n>
and --verbatim was not given; a LIVE session holds <old> in another window; two
rows for <old> differ only by case; the alias table would gain a cycle; or a
read could not be made.

Exit: the writer's own codes — 0 done · 1 environment · 2 refused · 3 committed,
not confirmed pushed · 4 mutex · 5 a move refused · 6 git.
USAGE
}

# ------------------------------------------------------------- the arguments
old=""; new=""; why=""; no_tmux="${LANE_RENAME_NO_TMUX:-0}"
pass=()
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
	case "$1" in
	-h | --help)  usage; exit 0 ;;
	--verbatim)   pass+=(--verbatim); shift ;;
	--no-github)  pass+=(--no-github); shift ;;
	--no-tmux)    no_tmux=1; shift ;;
	--why)        why="${2-}"; [ -n "$why" ] || die "--why needs a reason" 2; shift 2 ;;
	--why=*)      why="${1#--why=}"; [ -n "$why" ] || die "--why needs a reason" 2; shift ;;
	--)           shift ;;
	-*)           die "unknown option '$1' — see $prog --help" 2 ;;
	*)
		if   [ -z "$old" ]; then old="$1"
		elif [ -z "$new" ]; then new="$1"
		elif [ -z "$why" ]; then why="$1"
		else die "$prog takes <old> <new> [\"<why>\"] — '$1' is one argument too many. See $prog --help" 2
		fi
		shift ;;
	esac
done
[ -n "$old" ] && [ -n "$new" ] || { usage >&2; die "usage: $prog <old> <new> [\"<why>\"]" 2; }

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the writer
#
# Beside this command first — `--install` places both in one directory — then on
# PATH. One resolution, so a workstation with two copies never reads one and
# writes with the other.
LANES_EDIT="${LANES_EDIT:-}"
if [ -z "$LANES_EDIT" ]; then
	if [ -x "$selfdir/lanes-edit.sh" ]; then LANES_EDIT="$selfdir/lanes-edit.sh"
	else LANES_EDIT="$(command -v lanes-edit.sh 2>/dev/null || printf '')"
	fi
fi
[ -n "$LANES_EDIT" ] && [ -x "$LANES_EDIT" ] || die "no register writer at ${LANES_EDIT:-lanes-edit.sh} — it is installed beside this
    command by \`openRepoTools --install\`. Run that, or set \$LANES_EDIT." 1

have_tmux() {
	[ "$no_tmux" = 1 ] && return 1
	[ -n "${TMUX:-}" ] || return 1
	command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- this window
#
# READ BEFORE THE WRITE, and only read. Amendment 16(f) turns on ONE question —
# is this the lane's own window — and it is answered by the name this window
# carries NOW, which the write below is about to make stale.
#
# TWO SPELLINGS, AND NEITHER IS DERIVED FROM THE OTHER (Amendment 11(c), as
# `lane-start` states it): `#W` is the NAME, and `<session>:<@id>` is the ref
# the harness's own session records are matched on, which is the one
# `window-session` takes. `<session>:<index>` is a third spelling this command
# has no question for, so it is not read.
win_name=""; win_harness=""
if have_tmux; then
	win_name="$(tmux display-message -p '#W' 2>/dev/null || :)"
	win_harness="$(tmux display-message -p '#{session_name}:#{window_id}' 2>/dev/null || :)"
fi
# THIS WINDOW IS THE LANE'S WHEN IT CARRIES ITS NAME. Compared without case
# (Amendment 15) against BOTH names: `<old>`, which is the ordinary state, and
# `<new>`, which is the state a re-run leaves — a rename whose write landed and
# whose window rename did not is finished by running the same command again.
here=0
if [ -n "$win_name" ]; then
	[ "$(lc "$win_name")" = "$(lc "$old")" ] && here=1
	[ "$(lc "$win_name")" = "$(lc "$new")" ] && here=1
fi

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the write
#
# EVERY REFUSAL IS THE WRITER'S, INCLUDING THE ONE ABOUT THIS WINDOW. Clause (a)
# refuses a rename while a live session holds `<old>` elsewhere, and that read
# is `live-holder`'s, which knows whether a record is `here` — so this file does
# not ask the question twice and cannot answer it differently.
step "renaming lane $old → $new (lane-collision-protocol Amendment 16)"
rc=0
"$LANES_EDIT" rename-lane "$old" "$new" ${why:+"$why"} ${pass[@]+"${pass[@]}"} || rc=$?
if [ "$rc" != 0 ]; then
	# THE WRITER HAS ALREADY SAID WHY, in its own words and with the act to
	# take. Saying it again in different words is how two surfaces come to
	# disagree about one refusal; the only thing added here is that NOTHING of
	# this window's was touched, which is this file's own fact.
	case "$rc" in
	2) note "nothing was renamed here either: the window still carries '${win_name:-none}' and no pane was typed into." ;;
	*) note "the write exited $rc (the writer's own code, above). This window was NOT touched — read what it said before re-running: a 3 means the commit is local and may already be on origin." ;;
	esac
	exit "$rc"
fi

# --------------------------------------------------- clause (f): the two names
#
# THE WINDOW FIRST, because it is the half that needs nobody. `rename-window`
# also turns tmux's automatic-rename OFF for this window, which is what keeps
# the name through the next command that runs in it — `lane-start` takes the
# same call for the same reason.
if [ "$here" = 1 ] && have_tmux && [ "$win_name" != "$new" ]; then
	if tmux rename-window "$new" 2>/dev/null; then
		say "  window renamed '$win_name' → '$new'"
	else
		note "tmux would not rename this window, so it still carries '$win_name'. Rename it yourself: tmux rename-window $new"
	fi
fi

if [ "$here" != 1 ]; then
	# FROM ELSEWHERE, THE LANE'S OWN NEXT PROMPT FINISHES IT (clause (f)). This
	# is not a gap: the guard resolves the window's former name through the very
	# alias table this rename just wrote, renames the window itself and types the
	# `/rename`. What is printed is what a person would type if they got there
	# first.
	say ""
	say "  This is not lane $new's own window${win_name:+ (it is named '$win_name')}, so its window and its session are"
	say "  the lane's to fix — which the name guard does at its next prompt (Amendment 16(f)):"
	say "      tmux rename-window $new        # in the lane's own window"
	say "      /rename $new                   # typed in the lane's own session"
	exit 0
fi

# --------------------------------------------- the session name (Amendment 12(h))
#
# M1's CONDITIONS ARE THE ONES `guard_type` MAKES, and they are not a taste: a
# `/rename` typed at a shell is a command that does not exist, and typed into an
# editor it is text nobody wrote. So the pane is the one the LIVE RECORD names —
# `window-session` is the single implementation of that read (Amendment 11(h)) —
# and it is typed into only where its current command is `claude`.
#
# THE PANE IS NOT `$TMUX_PANE`. This command usually runs from a shell, and that
# shell's pane is running the shell: a person who typed `lane-rename` at a
# prompt is not typing into their own session, and the record is what says where
# the session actually is.
pane=""; sess_name=""
if have_tmux && [ -n "$win_harness" ]; then
	ws_out=""
	ws_out="$("$LANES_EDIT" window-session "$win_harness" 2>/dev/null)" || ws_out=""
	if [ -n "$ws_out" ]; then
		# `<sid><US><tmux><US><name><US><pid><US><profile>`, the record's own
		# fields — and the second is `<session>:<@id>.<%pane>`, a WINDOW target
		# with the pane on the end of it.
		#
		# THE PANE IS TAKEN OUT OF IT, AND ONLY FROM A TARGET THAT NAMES THIS
		# WINDOW — `guard_run`'s own rule, for the reason stated there: tmux
		# REUSES window ids, so a record placed in this window by its pid can
		# carry a target another window now answers to, and `/rename` typed
		# into that one would land in somebody else's pane. Where it does not
		# name this window the pane is left EMPTY and the line is printed.
		rec_tgt="$(printf '%s' "$ws_out" | awk -F'\037' '{print $2}')"
		sess_name="$(printf '%s' "$ws_out" | awk -F'\037' '{print $3}')"
		case "$rec_tgt" in none) rec_tgt="" ;; esac
		if [ -n "$rec_tgt" ] && [ "${rec_tgt%.*}" = "$win_harness" ]; then
			case "$rec_tgt" in *.%*) pane="${rec_tgt##*.}" ;; esac
		fi
	fi
fi

if [ -n "$sess_name" ] && [ "$(lc "$sess_name")" = "$(lc "$new")" ]; then
	say "  the session in this window is already named '$sess_name'"
	exit 0
fi

typed=0
if [ -n "$pane" ] && have_tmux; then
	cur="$(tmux display-message -p -t "$pane" '#{pane_current_command}' 2>/dev/null || :)"
	case "$cur" in
	claude)
		if tmux send-keys -t "$pane" "/rename $new" Enter 2>/dev/null; then
			typed=1
			say "  /rename $new typed into $pane (Amendment 12(h) M1: the only path a running session's name has)"
		fi ;;
	esac
fi
if [ "$typed" = 0 ]; then
	say ""
	say "  The session's own name is LOCKED to the lane (Amendment 12(h)) and no API renames a"
	say "  running one from outside it. Type this in lane $new's own session — or say nothing and"
	say "  let the name guard type it at that lane's next prompt:"
	say "      /rename $new"
fi
exit 0
